@node CMacs Redact
@chapter CMacs Redact: One Redaction Table for Every Model-Facing Surface

@cindex redaction
@cindex privacy rules
@cindex model-facing surfaces
@cindex cmacs-redact

The activity journal has a rule set that says what must not be kept:
exclude this window, drop that title, rewrite account numbers, hash a
project name (@pxref{Journal Filters}).  Those rules used to protect the
journal and nothing else.  The same window title still reached a model
through the window list, the screen inventory in a chat prompt, a
screenshot, the clipboard.

@code{cmacs-redact} makes that rule set the @emph{one} policy for
everything a model is shown.  There is no second rule format and no
second matcher: every function here builds an event in the journal's own
shape and hands it to the journal's filter engine.

Four things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{It is on by default.}  @code{cmacs-redact-mode} is a global
minor mode that starts enabled.  Nothing has to be configured for the
shipped rules (password managers, private browser windows,
authentication prompts, @file{.gpg} and @file{.authinfo} files,
@file{~/.ssh}) to apply.
@item
@strong{It does not need the journal.}  The rules are the journal's,
loaded on first use.  @code{cmacs-journal-mode} can stay off; nothing is
recorded by redacting.
@item
@strong{It fails closed.}  When the rules cannot be loaded, model-facing
surfaces show nothing until they can.
@item
@strong{It never says which rule matched.}  A refusal reads the same
whatever caused it.
@end itemize

And one thing it is not: @emph{a sandbox}.  An agent you have allowed to
evaluate Lisp, run a shell command or read arbitrary files can get at
anything you can.  Redaction covers the surfaces listed in
@ref{Redact Surfaces}; that table also says, line by line, what is not covered.

@lisp
;; The rules are the journal's.  Elisp:
(setq cmacs-journal-filters
      '((:match ((:app . "^org\\.keepassxc")) :exclude t)
        (:match ((:title . "Invoice"))
         :replace ((:title "[0-9]\\@{6,\\@}" "***")))
        (:match ((:text . "sk-[A-Za-z0-9]\\@{20,\\@}"))
         :replace ((:text "sk-[A-Za-z0-9]\\@{20,\\@}" "[api key]")))))
(cmacs-journal-filters-reload)
@end lisp

@example
# ~/.config/cmacs/journal-filters.yaml -- outside your tracked dotfiles
filters:
  - match: @{file: '/clients/'@}
    exclude: true
  - match: @{text: 'sk-[A-Za-z0-9]@{20,@}'@}
    replace: [@{field: text, regexp: 'sk-[A-Za-z0-9]@{20,@}', with: '[api key]'@}]
@end example

It is pure Elisp (@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-redact.el}) and has no
configure flag of its own: it ships wherever the journal's filter engine
does.

