@node Redact Policy
@section Who Counts as a Model

@cindex cmacs-redact-surfaces
@cindex cmacs-redact-as-model

The functions act only for a @dfn{model-facing} caller.  While
@code{cmacs-redact-mode} is on, a caller is model-facing when any of
these holds:

@table @code
@item tool
A brigade tool call is running: inside @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool} or
@code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool-async}, and, for an asynchronous call,
until it has delivered its result.
@item rpc
@code{inhibit-interaction} is bound, which cmacs's RPC dispatch does
around everything arriving over MCP, D-Bus, @command{emacsctl} and
bacon.
@item prompt
The code composing a model prompt said so with the macro
@code{cmacs-redact-as-model}.  @code{cmacs-ai-view} does.
@end table

@code{cmacs-redact-surfaces} is the list of those three symbols;
removing one stops redaction for that kind of caller and nothing else.

Otherwise---a command you ran, your own Lisp---nothing is redacted.  The
rules are about what leaves for a model, not about what you may see on
your own machine.

Three consequences worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Your own @command{emacsctl} from a terminal is redacted.}  Over
the RPC channel you are indistinguishable from a program, and the
program is assumed.  The journal makes the same assumption about its
rows.
@item
@strong{While an asynchronous tool call is in flight, everything is
treated as the tool's.}  An async tool finishes in a process sentinel,
far outside the call that started it, and that is exactly where OCR text
or a clipboard entry gets redacted.  A sentinel cannot be told from a
timer, so for those seconds a redaction function called from anywhere
acts.  It errs toward redacting.
@item
@strong{A chat turn is a model prompt even though you sent it.}  That is
what @code{cmacs-redact-as-model} is for: the screen inventory is
composed inside it.
@end itemize

Callers do not decide any of this.  They call the function;
@code{cmacs-redact-active-p} decides.

@node Redact Rules
@section What the Rules Mean on Each Kind of Thing

A rule is written against fields (@code{kind}, @code{app}, @code{title},
@code{pid}, @code{tags}, @code{monitor}, @code{buffer}, @code{file},
@code{mode}, @code{project}, @code{url}, @code{detail}, @code{text}).
Each kind of thing is put to the rules as the event the journal would
record for it, so a rule written for the journal matches here without
change.

@table @asis
@item Window
@code{kind} @samp{focus}, with @code{app}, @code{title}, @code{pid}.
@item Buffer
@code{kind} @samp{buffer}, @code{app} @samp{emacs}, with @code{buffer},
@code{file}, @code{mode}, @code{project}.  A Dired buffer's directory
and an indirect buffer's base file stand in as @code{file}.
@item Web page
A gsurf buffer is @code{kind} @samp{url}, @code{app} @samp{gsurf}, with
@code{url}, @code{title}, @code{buffer}, @code{mode}, and @code{detail}
@samp{container:NAME}.  A bare URL is @code{kind} @samp{url} with
@code{url} alone.
@item File
The buffer visiting it, when there is one; else @code{kind}
@samp{buffer} with @code{file} and the base name as @code{buffer}.
Judged under its own name and, for a local file, with symbolic links
resolved.
@item Free text
@code{kind} @samp{text}, with @code{text}.
@end table

What each action does:

@table @code
@item exclude
The thing is not shown at all.  An excluded window is not listed and
cannot be captured; an excluded buffer is not listed and cannot be read;
an excluded URL is @code{nil}.
@item drop
The field is blanked.  A window with its title dropped is still listed,
without a title.
@item replace
@itemx hash
The field is rewritten, exactly as the journal would store it.  Without
the journal's per-install key (it is created when the journal is first
turned on) a hash is keyed with a random key that lives only as long as
this Emacs.
@end table

One of this Emacs's own frames is a window to the compositor, titled
with the buffer in it.  Such a window is also excluded when a buffer on
that frame is, since the rules that hide the buffer are written on its
file or mode, not on a window title.

@subsection Free text

@cindex free text, redacting
@cindex cmacs-redact-text-field-rules

A clipboard entry, text read off the screen, a buffer inlined into a
prompt: none of it has fields.  @code{cmacs-redact-text} applies two
things, in this order.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Rules on the @code{text} field.}  The text is the event
@code{(:kind text :text STRING)}, so a rule matching on @code{text} does
to it what any rule does to its field: @code{exclude} replaces the whole
text with @samp{[withheld by your privacy rules]}, @code{drop} gives the
empty string, @code{replace} and @code{hash} rewrite it.  Write these
for things that are sensitive wherever they appear: API keys, private
key blocks, card numbers.
@item
@strong{What the other rules call sensitive inside a field}
(@code{cmacs-redact-text-field-rules}, on by default).  Every
@code{replace} entry is applied to the text whatever field it names and
whether or not its rule's match holds---free text has no fields to hold
it against.  And for every field a rule both matches on and hashes, each
occurrence of that match regexp in the text becomes its hash.  So a rule
that rewrites account numbers in window titles rewrites them in a
screenshot's text too, without being written twice.
@end enumerate

The limits of the second part, on purpose:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A regexp that can match the empty string (@samp{.*}, @samp{[0-9]*})
means ``the whole field'' where it was written and means nothing inside
a larger text.  It is skipped.
@item
A regexp anchored to the start or end of its field only matches at the
start or end of the whole text.
@item
An @code{exclude} or @code{drop} written for another field has nothing
in free text to act on.  A window being excluded does not remove its
title from OCR text of a screenshot; what removes it is that the
screenshot is refused (below).
@item
A rule whose match is a predicate function is only ever applied through
part 1.
@end itemize

Set @code{cmacs-redact-text-field-rules} to @code{nil} when a field rule
is too broad to be searched for in running text; then only @code{text}
rules touch free text.

YAML and Elisp stay in step: @code{text} is a field name like any other
in both (@samp{match: @{text: ...@}}, @samp{replace: [@{field: text,
...@}]}, @samp{:match ((:text . ...))}).  The journal never builds an
event with a @code{text} field, so such a rule changes nothing the
journal keeps.

@subsection Images

@cindex screenshots, refusing

An image cannot be rewritten the way a title can, so the answer is
whole: if something the rules exclude would be in the picture, there is
no picture.  The tool returns the error @samp{a window excluded by your
privacy rules is visible; nothing was captured}, the same words whatever
matched.

``On screen'' for a desktop capture means the windows on a tag some
monitor is showing, plus the buffers in this Emacs's visible frames.  A
password manager parked on a hidden tag does not block screenshots.
When the shown tags cannot be read, every window counts as on screen.  A
capture of a single named window asks about that window only, and an
excluded one is reported exactly like a pattern that matched nothing.

@node Redact Surfaces
@section Surfaces

@cindex surfaces covered by redaction

What is covered, and how.  ``Refused'' means the request fails without
saying why; ``rewritten'' means @code{drop}, @code{replace} and
@code{hash} are applied.

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .14 .52
@headitem Surface @tab Covered @tab How
@item Screen inventory (@code{cmacs-ai-view}): chat system prompt and
per-turn block, both libreclaw send paths, the @code{current_view} MCP
tool
@tab yes
@tab An excluded buffer is not listed, not counted, not inlined, not
remembered as ``the last one''.  Names, files and page titles are
rewritten.  Inlined text goes through the free-text rules.
@item @code{cmacs-ai-view-attach} (@kbd{C-c C-a})
@tab yes
@tab Same as the inventory, although it is your own command: what it
inserts is sent to a model.  Paste by hand to send something verbatim.
@item MCP resources @code{buffer://}, @code{file://}, @code{process://}
@tab yes
@tab An excluded buffer, file, or process buffer is refused (reported as
not found).  Text of a buffer or file that is @emph{not} excluded is
returned verbatim.
@item MCP resources @code{messages://}, @code{variable://}
@tab yes
@tab @code{messages://} is refused when @file{*Messages*} is excluded;
both pass their text through the free-text rules.  @code{variable://}
is refused for a sensitive variable (@pxref{Redact Variables}), reported
as not found.
@item Tools @code{describe_variable}, @code{list_hooks}
@tab yes
@tab A sensitive variable's value is never printed:
@code{describe_variable} answers with one fixed sentence,
@code{list_hooks} leaves it out.  Other values go through the free-text
rules.
@item MCP resource @code{screenshot://frame}, tool @code{gowl_screenshot}
@tab yes
@tab Refused while an excluded window or buffer is on screen; a
single-window capture asks about that window.
@item Tool @code{lrgterm_dump_screen}
@tab yes
@tab Refused while a visible buffer of the frame is excluded.
@item Tool @code{video_snapshot}
@tab yes
@tab Refused when the video plays in an excluded buffer; when its buffer
is unknown, while anything excluded is on screen.
@item Tools @code{gsurf_snapshot}, @code{gsurf_print_pdf}
@tab yes
@tab Refused for a page excluded by URL, title or buffer (checked in
@code{cmacs-gsurf-mcp-snapshot} / @code{-print-pdf}, which both the C
and Lisp tools reach).
@item gowl window lists, focused window, clipboard history,
@code{gowl_screen_text}
@tab by contract
@tab These tools are Lisp and call @code{cmacs-redact-window} /
@code{cmacs-redact-text}.  The calls live in their own files, not here.
@item The journal's LQL source @code{activity}
@tab yes
@tab Filtered before it is stored; hidden from agents unless
@code{cmacs-journal-expose-to-agents}.
@item @code{eval}, @code{crispy_eval}, @code{bacon_eval}, shell tools,
@code{execute_command}, @code{send_keys}, @code{cmacs_c_*},
@code{gi_call}
@tab @strong{no}
@tab These can compute anything, a sensitive variable's value included.
Not a sandbox; keep them out of an agent's allowlist if the rules matter.
@item D-Bus @code{Config.Get}, @code{Debug.DescribeVariable}, and the C
fallback of @code{describe_variable} (a build without the brigade)
@tab @strong{no, yet}
@tab Composed in C from a variable name; they do not ask the registry of
sensitive variables.  The one-line change each needs is to begin its
form with @code{(cmacs-redact-variable-guard NAME)}.
@item Buffer and file @emph{tools} (@code{list_buffers},
@code{get_buffer_content}, @code{search_buffer},
@code{project_read_file}, @dots{})
@tab @strong{only where the tool calls the contract}
@tab A tool that lists or reads buffers must call
@code{cmacs-redact-buffer-p} itself.  The C fallback handlers of tools
that have a Lisp replacement do not.
@item gsurf text tools (@code{gsurf_extract_text}, @code{gsurf_get_uri},
@code{gsurf_get_title}, @code{gsurf_eval_js}, @code{gsurf_list})
@tab @strong{only where the tool calls the contract}
@tab Same: @code{cmacs-redact-url} / @code{cmacs-redact-buffer-p} exist
for them.
@item LQL sources defined in Elisp (@code{buffers}, @code{windows},
@code{processes}, @code{gowl_clients}, @code{kill_ring}, @code{recentf},
@code{bookmarks}, @code{org_headings}, @code{notes},
@code{note_headings}, @code{note_links}, @code{note_properties}, and any
source a user or subsystem defines), through @code{lql_query},
@code{ai_lql}, an agent's LQL watch, D-Bus and @command{emacsctl lql}
@tab yes
@tab Redacted at the snapshot, once for every surface that can run a
query: rows of excluded buffers, files, notes, URLs and windows are
dropped, names rewritten, free-text columns through the free-text rules.
@xref{Redact LQL}.
@item LQL engine sources that are not defined in Elisp (files, git,
exec, journald, coredumps, k8s, SQL)
@tab @strong{no}
@tab The engine reads these itself, off the editor thread; nothing there
asks the rules.  A query can read a file the rules hide.
@item Brigade memory index and second-brain search over the notes repo
@tab @strong{no}
@tab Indexed text is not filtered by these rules.
@item D-Bus methods served from the capability table:
@code{Compositor.ListClients}, @code{.FocusedClient} and
@code{.FindClient} (what @command{emacsctl get clients} prints),
@code{Monitor.List}, @code{Edit.GetContent}, @code{.Search} and
@code{.GetOrgContent}, every @code{Gsurf} method,
@code{Debug.DescribeVariable} and @code{.ListHooks},
@code{Log.RecentMessages}, @code{WindowManager.Other}
@tab yes
@tab Rows of @file{cmacs-dbus-forward-table.h}: they call the tool, so
they show what it shows (@pxref{Capability D-Bus Legacy}).
@item D-Bus @code{BufferManager.*}, @code{FrameManager.List},
@code{WindowManager.SelectByBuffer}, @code{Project.ListFiles},
@code{.ReadFile} and @code{.Find}, @code{Search.Occur},
@code{Text.Line} and @code{.Delete}, @code{File.Recent},
@code{Clipboard.List}, @code{.Get} and @code{.GetSelection},
@code{Bookmark.List}, @code{Config.Get}
@tab yes
@tab Lisp method functions in @file{cmacs-dbus-forward.el} that call the
contract: an excluded buffer is absent (@code{Find} is false,
@code{PointFor} is -1, a read is the missing-buffer error), file names
go through @code{cmacs-redact-file}, clipboard text through
@code{cmacs-redact-text}, a sensitive variable is refused.
@item D-Bus @code{Compositor.Screenshot}, @code{Video.Snapshot}
@tab yes
@tab Refused by the capture rule; a single named window that is
excluded is ``no client matching pattern''.
@item D-Bus GNOME Shell search provider, the MPRIS track title, the
@code{Log.Line} signal
@tab yes
@tab An excluded buffer or file is not offered as a result (nor one
whose name a rule rewrites, since a result is opened by name); an
excluded buffer has no title; message text goes through the free-text
rules.
@item D-Bus @code{VC.Status}, @code{.Diff}, @code{.Log}, @code{.Blame}
@tab @strong{no}
@tab git's output for the repository, read by a subprocess: file names
and diffs of excluded files appear.
@item D-Bus @code{Events.Recent} and the @code{Events} signals, the
@code{Compositor} signals (@code{ClientAdded}, @code{FocusChanged},
@code{ClientTitleChanged}: app id and title)
@tab @strong{no}
@tab Broadcasts composed where the event happens, not for a caller.
Anything on the session bus can subscribe.
@item @code{keybinds://}; the bacon IPC and podomation gowl module's
client list and focused window; the MCP prompts that inline the client
list
@tab @strong{no}
@tab C that never runs Lisp: nothing there asks the rules.
@item @code{cad_snapshot}, @code{vidstudio_render_frame}, imgedit
renders
@tab @strong{no}
@tab Pictures of a document the agent is editing, not of the screen.
@item Text of a buffer or file that is not excluded
@tab @strong{no}
@tab Returned as it is when read on request; only free-text surfaces are
rewritten.  An agent editing a file needs the real text.
@item Files an agent reads with its own tools (a CLI agent's shell, its
own file reader)
@tab @strong{no}
@tab Outside cmacs.
@item Screen sharing through the portal, recordings, anything not
addressed to a model
@tab @strong{no}
@tab Out of scope.
@end multitable

@node Redact Variables
@section Sensitive Variables

@cindex sensitive variables
@vindex cmacs-redact-default-sensitive-variables
@vindex cmacs-redact-sensitive-variables

A variable has no window, buffer or file for a rule to name, so the
rules cannot say ``not this one''.  Two kinds of variable must never be
printed for a model all the same:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@emph{The rule set itself.}  What a person excludes is exactly what the
rule files are kept out of a dotfiles repository to protect, and nothing
may reveal the rule set or which rule matched.
@code{describe_variable cmacs-journal-filter--rules} used to print every
rule to any agent that asked.
@item
@emph{Holders of secrets}: an API key in an option, an auth-source
answer kept in a variable, the process environment.
@end itemize

So there is a registry, and every surface that prints a variable's value
asks it.

@defopt cmacs-redact-default-sensitive-variables
What ships: the journal's rule options
(@code{cmacs-journal-default-filters}, @code{cmacs-journal-filters},
@code{cmacs-journal-filter-files}), its hash key, everything named
@code{cmacs-journal-filter--@dots{}}, redaction's own copies of the
rules and key, these two lists themselves,
@code{cmacs-ai-search-api-key},
@code{cmacs-ai-openai-compatible-endpoints}, the GNU's Eye key tables,
@code{process-environment} and @code{initial-environment},
@code{auth-sources} and the auth-source caches, @code{password-data},
and any variable whose name contains @samp{api-key}, @samp{password} /
@samp{passwd}, @samp{secret}, @samp{credential} or an @samp{access} /
@samp{auth} / @samp{bearer} / @samp{refresh} @samp{-token}.
@end defopt

@defopt cmacs-redact-sensitive-variables
Yours.  Empty by default.
@end defopt

Both are lists of symbols (that variable) and strings (a regexp matched,
case-sensitively, against variable names), consulted together:

@lisp
(setq cmacs-redact-sensitive-variables
      '(my-work-vpn-settings "\\`my-private-"))
@end lisp

A subsystem that keeps a secret in a variable adds it with
@code{add-to-list}.  Leave the shipped list alone and add to your own,
so that a later version's additions reach you.

What follows for a sensitive variable, for a model-facing caller:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{describe_variable} answers @samp{This variable is withheld by your
privacy rules} and nothing else: not the value, not the documentation.
The sentence is the same for a variable that is bound, one that is void,
and a name that was never a variable, so it says nothing about what the
session holds.
@item
The @code{variable://} resource is refused the way an excluded buffer
is: reported as not found.
@item
@code{list_hooks} leaves the variable out.
@item
An @emph{alias} is as sensitive as what it names.
@item
@emph{It fails closed.}  An entry that is neither a symbol nor a valid
regexp makes every variable sensitive until it is fixed.
@end itemize

What it does not do:

@itemize @bullet
@item
It is not asked of you.  @kbd{M-x describe-variable} and your own Lisp
see everything; like the rest of this chapter it acts for model-facing
callers only (@code{cmacs-redact-active-p}).
@item
@emph{@code{eval} is out of scope.}  It is privileged: an agent you have
granted @code{eval} (or @code{crispy_eval}, a shell, @code{cmacs_c_*})
can read any variable, these included.  The registry protects the
read-only surfaces an agent gets by default.
@item
It protects variables by @emph{name}.  A secret copied into a variable
with an ordinary name is not found; the free-text rules (a @code{text}
rule for @samp{sk-@dots{}}) are what catch a value wherever it appears.
@end itemize

@node Redact LQL
@section LQL Sources

@cindex LQL sources, redacting
@findex cmacs-lql-set-source-redaction
@findex cmacs-lql-redact-rows

An agent that can run a query can read the editor-state sources, so they
are redacted where every query passes: the snapshot.  A Lisp source's
rows are turned into JSON on the editor thread just before a query is
submitted, inside the dynamic extent of whoever submitted it.  When that
is a model-facing caller the rows go through the rules first; your own
queries (@kbd{M-x cmacs-lql-query}, the REPL, a results buffer) never
do.

A source says what its columns @emph{are}, and the rules do the rest.
The declaration is the fifth argument of @code{cmacs-lql-define-source},
or @code{cmacs-lql-set-source-redaction} afterwards:

@lisp
(cmacs-lql-define-source
 "tickets" '(("file" string) ("dir" string) ("summary" string) ("state" string))
 #'my-ticket-rows "My tickets."
 '(("file" . file) ("dir" . derived) ("summary" . text) ("state" . plain)))
@end lisp

@multitable @columnfractions .16 .36 .48
@headitem Role @tab The column is @tab For a model
@item @code{buffer}
@tab A buffer name
@tab The row goes when the buffer is excluded; else the name as the
rules rewrite it.
@item @code{file}
@tab A file name
@tab The row goes when the file is excluded, judged by its name alone;
else the name as rewritten.
@item @code{url}
@tab A URL
@tab Likewise.
@item @code{app}, @code{title}, @code{pid}
@tab Together, one window
@tab The row goes when the window is excluded; else app and title as
rewritten.
@item @code{text}
@tab Free text (a string, or a list of strings)
@tab Through the free-text rules.
@item @code{derived}
@tab Computed from this row's buffer, file, URL or window: a directory,
a relative path, a base name
@tab Kept when the rules left those alone; blanked when they rewrote
one.
@item @code{plain}
@tab Not sensitive: a mode, a state, an id
@tab As it is.
@end multitable

Instead of an alist the declaration may be a function of the rows
(alists keyed by column name) returning the rows a model may see, for a
source whose rule is not a matter of roles; it can call
@code{cmacs-lql-redact-rows} for the part that is.  @code{windows} does:
its @code{frame} column is the title of one of this Emacs's frames.  Or
the symbol @code{none}, for rows that are already safe: the journal's
@code{activity} was filtered when it was recorded.

@strong{A source that declares nothing} is not left open.  In a
model-facing query every string in every one of its rows---in any
column, declared in the schema or not, and inside lists---goes through
the free-text rules.  Nothing can be dropped that way, since nothing
says which column names a file; declare the roles to get that.  In a
declared source an undeclared string column is @code{text} as well.

What keeps this safe and cheap:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{The two snapshots never meet.}  A source with a generation
function has its snapshot cached.  The cache is keyed by source name
@emph{and} by whether the snapshot was made for a model, so the user's
rows and a model's rows are different entries, and a model's entry also
carries the rule set's generation: new rules, new snapshot.
@item
@strong{It fails closed.}  When the rules are not usable, or a source's
redaction signals, a model's query sees that source with no rows.  You
still see yours.
@item
@strong{No disk.}  Files are judged by name: nothing is opened, no
symbolic link is followed, no project is looked up (one the editor
already knows is used).  Values are memoised across a snapshot, since a
column repeats itself down a table.  Redacting @code{buffers} costs a
few tenths of a millisecond per buffer.
@item
@strong{Nothing is counted.}  A dropped row leaves no placeholder and
the query is not told how many there were.
@end itemize

The limits: a @code{file} role does not see through a symbolic link
(reading the file is still refused, by @code{cmacs-redact-file-p});
@code{gowl_clients} rows now carry @code{app_id}, @code{title},
@code{pid}, @code{tags} and @code{floating} beside @code{client},
because a window cannot be judged by an opaque object; and the engine's
own sources are not Lisp and are not covered (see the table above).

@node Redact Fail Closed
@section Failing Closed

@cindex fail closed, redaction

The rule set is unusable when a listed YAML file exists but cannot be
read or parsed, or any rule holds a key, action, field or regexp the
engine does not understand (@pxref{Journal Filters}).  While it is:

@multitable @columnfractions .55 .45
@headitem Function @tab Answers
@item @code{cmacs-redact-window}, @code{cmacs-redact-url},
@code{cmacs-redact-event}, @code{cmacs-redact-buffer}
@tab @code{nil}: excluded
@item @code{cmacs-redact-buffer-p}, @code{cmacs-redact-file-p},
@code{cmacs-redact-capture-refused-p}
@tab non-@code{nil}: excluded
@item @code{cmacs-redact-text}
@tab @samp{[withheld: redaction rules failed to load]} (the empty string
stays empty)
@item @code{cmacs-redact-capture-guard},
@code{cmacs-redact-resource-guard}
@tab signal
@end multitable

So the inventory is empty, resources are refused, screenshots are
refused.  The warning that says which file and why is the journal's
(@file{*Warnings*}); fix it and run @kbd{M-x
cmacs-journal-filters-reload}.

A failed load is not retried on every call---that would re-read a broken
file thousands of times.  It is retried when a filter file changes: the
journal watches its files only while its mode is on, so redaction
compares their modification times itself, at most every
@code{cmacs-redact-check-interval} seconds (2).  A change to
@code{cmacs-journal-filters} in Elisp needs
@code{cmacs-journal-filters-reload}, as it always did.

The C callers fail closed too: each asks through the dispatch with a
form that begins @code{(require 'cmacs-redact)} and a guard that
signals, so a missing file, a broken guard and a refusal are the same
answer.

@node Redact Checking
@section Checking

@findex cmacs-redact-test

@kbd{M-x cmacs-redact-test} shows what a model would be given of what
you are looking at---the focused window under gowl, else the selected
buffer---as it is and as it would be shown.  With a prefix argument it
asks for the fields of a sample instead, and optionally a line of free
text, so you can check a rule against a title you would rather not have
on screen.  It shows the result and never which rule produced it.  It
answers as a model would see it even though you ran it.

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-filter-test} is the same question for the
journal.

@node Redact Options
@section Options

@multitable @columnfractions .38 .2 .42
@headitem Option @tab Default @tab Meaning
@item @code{cmacs-redact-mode}
@tab on
@tab Global minor mode.  Off: every function is the identity.
@item @code{cmacs-redact-surfaces}
@tab @code{(tool rpc prompt)}
@tab Which callers are model-facing.
@item @code{cmacs-redact-text-field-rules}
@tab @code{t}
@tab Field rules' @code{replace} entries and hashed match regexps also
apply to free text.
@item @code{cmacs-redact-resolve-projects}
@tab @code{t}
@tab Look a buffer's project up (once per buffer and directory, never
remote) for @code{project} rules.
@item @code{cmacs-redact-check-interval}
@tab 2.0
@tab Seconds between looks at whether a filter file changed.
@item @code{cmacs-redact-default-sensitive-variables}
@tab (@pxref{Redact Variables})
@tab Variables shipped as never printed for a model: symbols and name
regexps.
@item @code{cmacs-redact-sensitive-variables}
@tab @code{nil}
@tab Your own additions to that list.
@end multitable

The rules themselves are the journal's options:
@code{cmacs-journal-default-filters}, @code{cmacs-journal-filters},
@code{cmacs-journal-filter-files}, @code{cmacs-journal-filter-case-fold}.

@node Redact API
@section For Code That Shows Something to a Model

Put @code{(declare-function cmacs-redact-... "cmacs-redact")} at top
level and @code{(require 'cmacs-redact)} in the body, then just call.
Do not test who is asking.

@defun cmacs-redact-window app title &optional pid
@code{nil} when the window must not be shown; else @code{(@var{app}
. @var{title})}, either half possibly rewritten or @code{nil}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-buffer-p buffer-or-name
Non-@code{nil} when the buffer must not be listed or read.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-buffer buffer-or-name
@code{nil} when excluded; else a plist (@code{:buffer} @code{:file}
@code{:mode} @code{:project}, @code{:url} @code{:title}) to print from.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-file-p file
Non-@code{nil} when the file must not be read.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-file file
For listing: @code{nil} when excluded, else the name, possibly
rewritten.  By name only; never touches the disk.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-buffer-name name
@code{nil} when excluded, else the name, possibly rewritten.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-generation
A value that changes when redacted output could: for the key of a cache
of redacted things.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-url url
@code{nil} when excluded; else the URL, possibly rewritten.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-text string
The string with the free-text rules applied; never @code{nil} for a
string.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-event plist
The general form: the filtered plist, or @code{nil}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-capture-refused-p what &optional target
Non-@code{nil} when an image must not be captured.  @var{what} is one of
@code{desktop}, @code{all}, @code{window}, @code{client}, @code{frame},
@code{buffer}, @code{gsurf}, @code{video}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-capture-guard what &optional target
The same, signalling.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-resource-guard kind &optional name
Signals unless the MCP resource may be read.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-variable-p symbol
Non-nil when a variable (a symbol or a name) must not be printed: answer
with @code{cmacs-redact-variable-refusal} and nothing else.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-variable-guard symbol
The same, signalling: what a handler written in C begins its form with.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-redact-sensitive-variable-p symbol
The registry alone, whoever is asking.
@end defun

@defmac cmacs-redact-as-model body@dots{}
@var{body} composes something a model will read.
@end defmac

Rules for a new surface:

@itemize @bullet
@item
List only what @code{cmacs-redact-window} / @code{cmacs-redact-buffer-p}
let through, and print the rewritten values, not the originals.
@item
Never report that something was left out: no ``3 windows hidden'', no
placeholder row.  A count is a fact about the rule set.
@item
Never put a rule, a regexp or a rule number in an error.
@item
A surface that prints the value of a variable chosen by the caller asks
@code{cmacs-redact-variable-p} first, by name, before looking at whether
the variable is bound.
@item
Text produced later (a sentinel, a timer) by an async brigade tool is
covered by the @code{tool} surface.  Text produced later by anything
else is not: wrap its composition in @code{cmacs-redact-as-model}.
@item
A tool handler written in C asks through the dispatch, with the form
beginning @code{(require 'cmacs-redact)} and names escaped by
@code{cmacs_dispatch_lisp_escape}.
@end itemize

@node Redact Internals
@section Internals

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{One engine.}  @file{cmacs-redact.el} calls
@code{cmacs-journal-filter-apply} for events and
@code{cmacs-journal-filter-apply-text} for free text; both live in
@file{cmacs-journal-filter.el}.  The only change to the engine is the
@code{text} field and the free-text function.
@item
@strong{One event shape.}  A buffer is described by
@code{cmacs-journal--buffer-event}, the function the journal records it
with.  Loading @file{cmacs-journal.el} for that turns nothing on.
@item
@strong{The advice.}  @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool} gets an around
advice that binds a variable; @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool-async} gets
one that also wraps the callback, so the call counts as in flight until
that function's exactly-once callback runs.  A lost callback is
forgotten after fifteen minutes.
@item
@strong{Loaded from inside a tool call.}  Callers require the file in a
function body, so the first load may happen inside a tool call the
advice was too late to wrap.  The file looks at the backtrace when it
loads and, if it finds a brigade call on it, counts that call as in
flight for a minute.
@item
@strong{The hash key is never changed here.}
@code{cmacs-journal-filter-hash-key} is bound, not set, around each
application, so a session key standing in for a missing journal key can
never end up in a journal file.
@item
@strong{LQL.}  @file{cmacs-lql.el} redacts in
@code{cmacs-lql--snapshot-1} and keys @code{cmacs-lql--snapshot-cache}
by @code{(NAME . REDACTED)}.
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-lql-redact-tests.el} covers the roles, the
default, fail closed, that the two snapshots never share an entry or a
generation, and that every shipped source declares every string column.
@item
@strong{Tests.}  @file{test/cmacs/cmacs-redact-tests.el}: each contract
function against rules; fail closed; who is asking, including the async
window; the screen inventory; captures and resources; that no result or
error contains the rule; and a source guard that every C image handler
and resource handler still asks first.
@end itemize
